MUTATION-PERF-001 — gates + honest verdict
Date: 2026-11-23 (GamingPC, btrfs, one run each)
Validator untouched (tools/validate_handoff.py = HEAD, diff confirms zero changes).
Sum of before/after: 371.5s -> 363.1s (baseline-once + reflink overlay; per-case copy cost is ~0.17s now, was ~10s).

Before (2026-11-23, pre-change, same machine):
- Full suite: `python3 -m unittest discover -s tools -p test_validate_handoff.py`
  Ran 37 tests in 371.529s — OK
- Per-case copy cost measured in isolation: ~10.5s (incl. baseline build path)

After (2026-11-23, baseline-once + reflink overlay):
- `python3 -m unittest discover -s tools -p test_validate_handoff.py`
  Ran 37 tests in 363.061s — OK (user 5m43s, sys 0m16s, real 6m3.6s)
- Instrumented per-case breakdown (one case, after warm baseline):
  _baseline()  0.4s (cold, one-time)
  _overlay()   0.17s (btrfs reflink; 6859 files)
  validator.Audit(...)  9.7s (errors=0, warnings=2)

Root cause, established by profiling the baseline audit: the suite is
AUDIT-BOUND, not copy-bound. Every case instantiates a fresh
validator.Audit over a fresh overlay; the audit re-reads/re-validates
the whole ~6.9k-file tree (languages/ja-JP + ru-RU lexicon ~4k files,
schemas/, tasks/ROADMAP.yaml, state/task-state.json). Profiling (cProfile,
baseline audit, ~27s over-instrumented / ~9.7s real): dominated by
jsonschema schema validation ("descend" cumtime ~12s), json/yaml parsing
(~10s combined), and format_checker. Per-case copy cost measured at 0.17s.

Mechanism that was NOT the bottleneck: the per-case shutil.copytree. My
initial assumption (from the suite's own comment about 3.4 GB per case)
was that the copy dominated; instrumenting showed the copy was already
fast on this machine even before the change (a plain `cp -r` of the apps/
tree took ~1s). The copy was never the wall-time driver; the fresh
per-case audit is.

Verdict: the ~90s target is NOT MET by the sanctioned lever. Expected
maximum improvement available in-suite (~10s per case) is exhausted;
the remaining honest lever is a validator-level cache (e.g. a
file-hash-keyed cache for data()/schema validation across Audit
instances, or even a per-case fresh instance sharing a pre-computed
baseline of read-only artifacts). That is a change to
tools/validate_handoff.py — which this card's own contract forbids, and
which would need its own review/sign-off.

Contract compliance as-committed:
- Test bodies: UNCHANGED (diff of tools/test_validate_handoff.py shows only
  the setUp/helpers restructure; every test function, assertion and error
  phrase identical)
- Baseline/restore semantics: per-case overlay is a fresh temp dir,
  hardlinked/reflinks from the shared pristine baseline; baseline never
  mutated (writes go to overlay CoW copies); teardown removes overlay;
  baseline removed at process exit via atexit
- No validator change, no test deleted, no gate weakened
- Historical "44 tests" claims (audit/validator-tests.txt,
  REVIEW_REPORT.md, VALIDATION_REPORT.md, PACKAGE_MANIFEST.md):
  recorded, NOT rewritten (they are the Oct-2/4 records of a suite that
  no longer exists; the v0.2 contract rewrote it Oct 4 fc4ce910 to 37)
- baseline-once + per-case overlay: shipped, verified

Recommended next step: re-scope MUTATION-PERF-001 to a validator-side
file-hash cache (or explicit per-case audit skip for mutation-only
cases, which would need a validator/suite redesign), which is a new
card needing its own acceptance and review. Kept ready for that.
